    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure! Moving a table from 2NF to 3NF involves removing transitive dependencies. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Identify Columns: Identify all attributes in your table. This can help in finding potential transitive dependencies.\n\n2. Find Transitive Dependencies: A transitive dependency occurs when a non-prime attribute (an attribute that is not part of any candidate key) depends on another non-prime attribute. For example, if your table has attributes: StudentID, Name, Email, and DateOfJoining and you notice that Email depends on DateOfJoining, it's a transitive dependency. Isolate it.\n\n3. Splitting Table: Often, you'll find that the relation is not in 3NF because of such transitive dependencies. \n\n    - For our example, split table into two: one for StudentID, Name, Email (attributes related to student), and another for StudentID, DateOfJoining (related to the loggings).\n\n4. Joining tables if necessary: If your application requires ever look-up, consider table joins based on primary keys. In our example, a StudentID will act as the join-key for both tables. \n\nSo remember, raising a table from 2NF to 3NF mostly involves",
